<h1>A word-frequency counter</h1>

<p>This little program reads text from standard input and prints how many
words it saw. It is written as a literate program: the narrative below is
the source of truth, and weft <em>tangles</em> it into
<code>wordcount.c</code> and <em>weaves</em> this very page.</p>

<h2>The shape of the program</h2>

<p>Every C program is a few includes, some state, and a
<code>main</code> that drives it. We name each part and assemble them,
so <code>main</code> reads like a summary you can expand on demand:</p>

@o wordcount.c @{
@<Includes@>

int main(void) {
    @<Program state@>
    @<Count the words@>
    @<Report the total@>
    return 0;
}
@}

<p>The rest of the chapter fills in each named fragment.</p>

<h2>State and includes</h2>

<p>We need the standard I/O and character-classification headers, and a
single counter. Both fragments start here and may grow later as the
program does.</p>

@d Includes @{
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>
@}

@d Program state @{
long words = 0;      /* words seen so far */
int  in_word = 0;    /* are we inside a word right now? */
@| words in_word @}

<h2>Counting</h2>

<p>A word is a maximal run of non-space characters. We walk the input one
character at a time and count each <em>transition</em> from outside a word
to inside one &mdash; that way runs of spaces never inflate the total.</p>

@d Count the words @{
int c;
while ((c = getchar()) != EOF) {
    if (isspace(c)) {
        in_word = 0;
    } else if (!in_word) {
        in_word = 1;
        words++;
    }
}
@}

<h2>Reporting</h2>

<p>Finally we print the total. The reporting fragment is deliberately
tiny &mdash; but suppose we also want a friendly message when the input
was empty. We <em>grow</em> the same fragment with a second piece; weft
marks the continuation with <code>+&equiv;</code> and lists both parts in
the footer.</p>

@d Report the total @{
printf("%ld words\n", words);
@}

@d Report the total @{
if (words == 0)
    printf("(no input)\n");
@}

<h2>Build and run</h2>

<p>Referencing @<Count the words@> and @<Report the total@> from the
prose gives clickable links to their definitions. To build:</p>

<pre><code class="language-sh">weft wordcount.weft        # tangle -> wordcount.c
cc -o wc wordcount.c
echo "one two three" | ./wc
3 words</code></pre>

<h2>Indices</h2>

<h3>Fragments</h3>
@m

<h3>Identifiers</h3>
@u
